  "query_preview": "..A broad trough currently is currently centered over\nthe Pacific Northwest, with the associated frontal boundary over\neastern Nevada this morning. In moisture and instability on the\nleading edge of the system, precipitation has already begun over\nthe western half of Utah, with snow levels in the 5500-6500 foot\nrange. Thus, mountain snow is already accumulating. This system\nwill come through Utah and southwest Wyoming in pieces through\nearly Saturday. \n\nLight and steady precipitation is expected to continue during the\nday today, with a burst of heavier valley rain/mountain snow\ntonight into early Friday as the frontal boundary crosses the\narea. Behind the front, precip will become primarily focused over\nnorthern Utah where the best cold pool instability will remain. As\nthe main trough tracks across the area, temperatures and thus snow\nlevels will steadily lower during the day Friday, bringing snow\nlevels down to northern Utah valley floors late Friday into early\nSaturday, bringing the potential for accumulating snow. Limiting\nfactor on those accumulations will be the duration of\nprecipitation after snow levels lower sufficiently and that rates\nwill be generally lower during this time. \n\nOverall, guidance has been trending upward with snowfall totals\nover the last several runs, particularly over northern Utah. With\ntwo feet of snow now in the forecast for portions of the Wasatch\nincluding the Upper Cottonwoods, have upgraded both the northern\nand southern Wasatch mountain zones to a Winter Storm Warning\ngiven anticipated impacts with other headlines unchanged at this\ntime. \n\nBehind the exit trough, confidence is high that high pressure will\ntrack across Utah and southwest Wyoming Saturday into Sunday,\nbringing dry conditions. With the clearing and unseasonably cool\nairmass, freezing temperatures will be in play for lower elevation\nportions of southern Utah, with the best chance in the Virgin\nRiver corridor of Zion National Park. This remains more likely\nFriday night/Saturday morning than on Sunday morning. Temperatures\nin the vicinity of St. George are also expected to get close to\nfreezing, though there is a relatively low chance of temperatures\nmoving to or below freezing overnight during that period.\n\nWith no blocking ridge developing off the Pacific coast, this\nbreak in the action will be short lived, with increasing\nconfidence that an active pattern will persist for much of the\nupcoming week. The next storm system is expected to move through\nMonday into Tuesday. Latest guidance is leaning toward a solution\nthat is noticeably weaker than the last couple of systems,\nbringing minor impacts to primarily southern Utah. Beyond that,\nthere is the potential for a stronger system to move through for\nthe middle to latter part of next week.",
